---
import Base from "@layouts/Base.astro";
import Avatar from "@components/Avatar.astro";
import { CATEGORIES, tools_read_published } from "@data/tools.js";

const AVATAR_SIZE_ABOUT = 96;

// About이 막다른 골목이 되지 않게 한다. 도구가 없으면 카테고리로 내보낸다.
const published = tools_read_published();

// 아직 발행된 도구가 없는 카테고리. **빈 배열이 될 수 있다** — 세 카테고리에
// 하나씩 발행되는 순간이 그렇다. 그때 "The rest of the sections:" 문구 자체가
// 나오면 안 된다. 안 그러면 화면에 "… . The rest of the sections: ." 이 찍힌다.
const remaining = CATEGORIES.filter((c) => published.every((t) => t.category !== c.key));
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="Who I am, why I make these, and how I check the numbers."
>
  <h1>About</h1>

  <div class="author-card">
    <Avatar size={AVATAR_SIZE_ABOUT} />
    <div>
      <p class="author-name">Planimeter</p>
      <p class="author-line">
        A pen name. I build and validate numerical models, and these are the same
        habit at hobby scale.
      </p>
      <ul class="author-facts">
        <li>Every page names its model and what that model assumes.</li>
        <li>The maths sits in its own file, with unit tests on it.</li>
        <li>Nothing here is advice, and no page tells you what to do.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    I make small interactive tools here. You move the parameter that matters, a
    chart moves, and you get to see the shape of the thing instead of one number.
  </p>

  <p>
    Most of that work is finding out where a model stops being trustworthy, which
    is more or less what this whole site is about.
  </p>

  <p>
    A planimeter is a mechanical instrument that measures the area under a curve
    by tracing its outline. It is a calculating device from before calculators:
    you move it by hand and read the answer off a dial. That is roughly what these
    tools are, and the name is a reminder that the reading is only as good as the
    instrument.
  </p>

  <h2>What these are</h2>

  <p>
    Toys for thinking with. Each one runs a simple model and draws what it says.
    A hobby, mostly — I make them because I want to see the curve myself, and
    putting them online costs nothing extra. There is no company behind it and no
    plan beyond finishing the next one.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you are going to act on a number from one of these, check it somewhere
    else too.
  </p>

  <h2>How I check the numbers</h2>

  <p>
    Each tool cites its formula to something you can open. Where I could not
    verify a source, the page says so instead of linking to a good guess.
  </p>

  <p>
    Where I can, I check a formula against a second version I wrote separately,
    because running the same model twice is not a check.
  </p>

  <p>
    Every page also lists what its model leaves out. That is usually the most
    useful part.
  </p>

  <h2>Getting things wrong</h2>

  <p>
    I will. If you find an error I would genuinely like to know — the <a
    href="/contact">contact page</a> has my email. I fix them and note what
    changed on the page, with the date. Typos I just fix quietly.
  </p>

  <h2>Copying</h2>

  <p>
    The writing, the diagrams and the code are mine. Read them, link to them,
    quote a bit with credit, use the tools for whatever you like. Please don't
    republish the articles wholesale or lift the calculators onto another site.
    If you want to do something bigger with them, just ask.
  </p>

  <p>
    The maths belongs to nobody. Formulas come from published sources and are
    cited on the page that uses them.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to start</h2>

  <p>
    {published.length > 0 && (
      <>
        Finished so far:{" "}
        {published.map((t, i) => (
          <>
            {i > 0 && ", "}
            <a href={`/${t.slug}`}>{t.name}</a>
          </>
        ))}.
      </>
    )}
    {remaining.length > 0 && (
      <>
        {published.length > 0
          ? " The rest of the sections: "
          : "The sections, each with its own writing: "}
        {remaining.map((c, i) => (
          <>
            {i > 0 && (i === remaining.length - 1 ? " and " : ", ")}
            <a href={c.href}>{c.name}</a>
          </>
        ))}.
      </>
    )}
  </p>

  <p class="note">
    Everything here is provided as is, with no promise that it's accurate or
    working. It's a hobby site.
  </p>
</Base>
